WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:06.044
When a community wants to build

00:00:06.044 --> 00:00:10.257
a bridge or transit system, housing,

00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:11.883
sustainable infrastructure --

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:13.635
where does the money come from?

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.930
Money ultimately decides what gets built.

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:18.348
Money decides what survives.

00:00:18.348 --> 00:00:21.768
Money decides what future
becomes possible.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.104
So change where money goes
and who controls it --

00:00:24.104 --> 00:00:26.856
then you change what becomes possible.

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:28.900
I'm Trinity Tran,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.445
I lead efforts to build
public banks in California and beyond.

00:00:32.946 --> 00:00:35.699
Most of the time, our cities
don’t have the money upfront

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.745
to be able to pay for a project
like a bridge or housing.

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:44.916
And so they will issue a bond,

00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:47.085
which is essentially a long-term loan,

00:00:47.085 --> 00:00:50.130
provided [and] structured
by Wall Street banks.

00:00:50.171 --> 00:00:55.010
A loan is money that is provided upfront
and paid back over time with interest.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.097
Wall Street banks -- private lenders --
end up tacking on fees

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:00.765
to structure and sell those bonds.

00:01:00.807 --> 00:01:04.102
Then the reality is that our local
governments are not only paying

00:01:04.144 --> 00:01:05.770
for the cost of the project,

00:01:05.812 --> 00:01:08.898
but decades of interest on top of that.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.651
So for every dollar that we're borrowing,

00:01:11.693 --> 00:01:15.989
we're paying another dollar
in interest over time, sometimes more.

00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:19.784
And that's all money that's going
out the door to private lenders.

00:01:19.826 --> 00:01:22.203
We're paying hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:23.705
in the city of Los Angeles.

00:01:23.705 --> 00:01:28.043
At the state level, in California,
we’re paying $4 billion

00:01:28.084 --> 00:01:30.128
in interest annually.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.423
That's money flowing out
of our communities

00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:35.800
rather than being invested locally.

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:39.304
Private banks are owned by shareholders.

00:01:39.346 --> 00:01:42.098
They're investing in areas
that make them a lot of money,

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.311
and not necessarily what’s
in the best interest of our communities.

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:48.980
Public banking asks a different question:

00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:52.984
how do we do more with the dollars
that our communities generate

00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:55.362
without raising taxes,

00:01:55.403 --> 00:01:58.031
but by lowering the cost
of financing itself?

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:01.368
A public bank is a bank
owned by the people

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:05.163
through their local government,
either city or region or state.

00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:09.709
Every time you buy something,
like a cup of coffee,

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:13.672
a portion of that tax will go
to your city and your state.

00:02:13.672 --> 00:02:17.801
So we're using the money that we already
generate that belongs to us.

00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:21.179
They don't have shareholders to pay.

00:02:21.179 --> 00:02:24.182
So as loans are repaid,
it comes back into a public bank.

00:02:24.182 --> 00:02:28.353
Its mission is to support
the local economic development

00:02:28.353 --> 00:02:29.896
of the community it serves.

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:32.273
That would be designed at a local level.

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:36.069
So the law that we created --
the California Public Banking Act --

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:39.114
created the legal framework
for cities and municipalities

00:02:39.114 --> 00:02:40.824
to be able to form their own banks.

00:02:40.865 --> 00:02:45.245
And so the mission of the bank,
the mandates, the lending priorities,

00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:48.206
the investment priorities,
the capitalization strategies,

00:02:48.248 --> 00:02:50.333
all the details of how
this is going to work

00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:53.128
is going to be defined at a local level.

00:02:53.878 --> 00:02:58.174
There are 900 public banks globally

00:02:58.216 --> 00:03:01.010
with nearly $50 trillion in assets.

00:03:01.052 --> 00:03:04.222
In Germany, a network
of 400 municipal banks

00:03:04.222 --> 00:03:08.727
was able to support the country's
renewable energy transition.

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:12.355
In the United States,
we have a successful model to emulate.

00:03:12.397 --> 00:03:13.773
The Bank of North Dakota

00:03:13.815 --> 00:03:18.153
is a 100-year-old public bank
founded in 1919.

00:03:18.153 --> 00:03:20.989
It was started by socialist farmers

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:26.077
who were being gouged
by out-of-state New York bankers.

00:03:26.077 --> 00:03:27.454
During the Great Depression,

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:31.666
nearly 200,000 farms
were being foreclosed on.

00:03:31.708 --> 00:03:34.294
Because the state of North Dakota
had a public bank,

00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:37.922
it was able to step in and buy the farms

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:40.592
and lend it back
to the families for a dollar.

00:03:41.092 --> 00:03:44.262
Once the families were able to stabilize,

00:03:44.262 --> 00:03:48.057
they sold it back
at a price they could afford.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.601
For the last century,

00:03:49.642 --> 00:03:52.312
it has supported local
economic development,

00:03:52.353 --> 00:03:55.106
supported small businesses, infrastructure

00:03:55.106 --> 00:03:59.944
while still returning very healthy
return on equity, 15 to 18 percent.

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:03.156
That means it's returning
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:06.242
back into the general fund
year after year.

00:04:06.785 --> 00:04:10.663
So that's an example of how a public bank
is able to step in and support

00:04:10.705 --> 00:04:13.082
and strengthen the community

00:04:13.124 --> 00:04:16.461
without focusing on just purely profit.

00:04:16.503 --> 00:04:20.423
Los Angeles just dealt
with a devastating wildfire.

00:04:20.423 --> 00:04:21.758
So if we had a public bank,

00:04:21.758 --> 00:04:26.387
it would have been able to step in
to offer immediate, low-cost loans.

00:04:26.429 --> 00:04:30.934
It's able to be immediately responsive
to the needs of the community

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:33.561
without having to wait for federal aid,

00:04:33.603 --> 00:04:36.481
which can be unstable.

00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:39.484
We're moving towards building a system

00:04:39.526 --> 00:04:44.697
that mobilizes public revenue
to support our public priorities.

00:04:44.739 --> 00:04:48.034
We've seen such an incredible
amount of progress

00:04:48.076 --> 00:04:49.369
in such a short time,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:54.332
because even without a complex
understanding of finance and banking,

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:56.709
when you start explaining public banking,

00:04:56.709 --> 00:05:01.130
it makes sense on a logical,
intuitive level.

00:05:01.172 --> 00:05:04.384
Multiple cities and states
are working towards it.

00:05:04.384 --> 00:05:07.095
We have the ability
to create a new system.

00:05:07.136 --> 00:05:10.306
This isn't a pie-in-the-sky idea.

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:14.936
This is a model that works for the people,

00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:17.689
our communities and our environment.

